WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
SPEAKER: In GKE,
application owners

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.170
can define the minimum
and maximum amount

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.500
of CPU and memory that
their workloads require.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.470
This is done in the form of per
container requests and limits

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.390
in a Kubernetes pod.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.280
This process isn't
perfect though.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.290
Workload requirements
may need to change over

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:19.080
time for many reasons,
from incoming demand

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.190
on the applications to
improper initial configuration.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.920
App right-sizing is a practice
of fine tuning workload

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.520
requests over time to ensure
that their requests accurately

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.460
reflect what the
workloads will utilize.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.670
In order to make GKE cost
optimization a discipline,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.572
it's important to
provide a monitoring

00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:39.780
system that can help teams
practice app right-sizing.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.186
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:49.800
Previously, we
discussed how it is

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.080
important to monitor
cluster bin packing,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.500
or how efficiently resources
are distributed at a cluster

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.360
and node pool level.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
Signals of poor utilization
at these aggregated levels

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.730
can be indicative of
applications being

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:05.290
over or under provisioned.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.210
Monitoring at the
application level

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.190
can empower application
owners to have

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.800
the right data to
right-size their apps

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.890
and put GKE cost
optimization into practice.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.780
To monitor this
effectively, we want

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.520
to ensure that
clusters utilize name

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.170
spaces in a logical fashion.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.080
For many reasons, we do not
want to have a single namespace

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.880
running all pods.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.820
These include overly permissive
role-based access control,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:34.910
inability ability to set quotas
for team, the list goes on.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.740
But from a monitoring
perspective,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.830
we want to be able
to utilize namespaces

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.890
to understand app right-sizing
patterns across groups

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.600
of applications.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.210
A common example of how
this could be useful

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.730
would be for namespaces to
be aligned to each team.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.630
This easily allows us to
then chart and monitor

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.320
how each team is doing.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.650
For example, when a
team is consistently

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:00.670
overprovisioned on a specific
resource across multiple apps,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.590
we can make that information
available to them.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.980
This builds a culture
around empowering teams

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.070
to right-size their apps.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:11.140
The way we can detect
how well they're doing,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.940
is by utilizing
platform-specific

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.170
recommendations.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:17.760
For example, the
vertical pod autoscaler.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.770
This means that we'll
get recommended values

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.080
for CPU and memory
requests, which

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.510
we can chart against
the actual resources

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:28.650
that pods are requesting
in that given namespace.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.920
Once we have a team to work
with on right-sizing apps,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.220
we can then dig into monitoring
for their overprovisioned apps.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.070
The data we've already collected
for the aggregate namespace

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.430
view will be utilized here as
well, this time on a per work

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.080
load basis.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.630
Here, we can see that the
front end and back end

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.260
workloads for team A are
well over provisioned.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.790
So what can we do now?

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.070
We can start with auto
generated recommendations

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.110
from vertical pod autoscaler.

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.910
This may give us
quick, easy guidance

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.210
to right-size overprovisioned
apps by reducing CPU and memory

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:04.530
requests.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:07.230
We should also create per
application dashboards

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.820
where we graph out
data, including

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.700
what resources the
pods are utilizing,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.580
what the sum of requests and
limits for those pods are,

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.440
and how their
utilization compares

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.740
to their target utilization
defined by a horizontal pod

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.240
autoscaler.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.390
Note that in this example,
we have the horizontal pod

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.420
autoscaler's target
utilization for a set of pods

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.270
well above actual utilization.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.835
This introduces an opportunity
for cost optimization

00:03:32.835 --> 00:03:36.060
by app right-sizing.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.180
When we have average utilization
well below the target

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.660
utilization defined in the
horizontal pod autoscaler,

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.870
we can consider lowering the
number of minimum replicas.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:47.880
By reducing this
count, we get to reduce

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.770
the cost of running
this application,

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.410
all while maintaining
flexibility to still scale up

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:54.840
to handle varying load.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:57.210
While we have mainly
focused on cost optimization

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.310
with the goal of
reducing cluster costs,

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:00.810
it's a good time
to remind ourselves

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.850
that cost optimization
and practicing

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.160
FinOps is the
discipline of balancing

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.530
cost with other critical areas.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.690
With this same data,
we can also monitor

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.833
for under provisioned
apps as well,

00:04:11.833 --> 00:04:14.250
which are opportunities for
right-sizing apps for the sake

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.620
of performance and stability.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.440
Over time, you'll find that
cluster bin packing and app

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.750
right-sizing are
intertwined exercises

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.540
due to events such as the
onboarding of entirely new apps

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.390
into a cluster.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.240
Configuring a monitoring system
around the topics discussed

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.910
here can be complex, so be
sure to check out the guide we

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.700
published on how to set one up.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.410
With a system set up for
cluster bin packing and app

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.620
right-sizing, you can
ensure that your team will

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:44.250
be able to continuously practice
cost optimization for your GKE

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.800
clusters.

